			'show' => array('st_augustine'=>'1','bermuda'=>'1','zoysia'=>'0'),
			'faqs' => array(
				array('question'=>'Why is Richmond soil so variable across a single property?','answer'=>'Because Richmond sits in the Brazos River bottomlands, where centuries of river deposition have created a layered, non-uniform soil profile. Sandy loam in one part of a lot can transition to compacted clay 30 feet away. We dig 3–5 soil probes across every Richmond lot before quoting, and we calibrate the amendment depth and grass-variety selection to each zone rather than running a single Richmond spec. Treating a Richmond lot as uniform is the single biggest source of failed Richmond installs.'),
				array('question'=>'Do you handle floodplain and drainage compliance on Brazos-adjacent properties?','answer'=>'Yes — and on lower-elevation Richmond lots, drainage compliance is the entire conversation before we discuss grass varieties. We review existing storm-water plans, coordinate elevation with civil-engineering documentation when required, and install French drains tied into existing infrastructure. Flood-tolerant grass-variety selection (St. Augustine leans more flood-tolerant than Bermuda) is calibrated to the specific elevation and drainage profile of your lot.'),
				array('question'=>'What grass works best in Richmond?','answer'=>'On established Richmond lots with mature canopy (Pecan Grove, Greatwood, older Long Meadow Farms filings), Palmetto St. Augustine — it handles the 4–5 hours of filtered light most established Richmond lots actually get, tolerates Richmond soil once installed over an amended surface, and is more flood-tolerant than Bermuda on lower-elevation lots. On full-sun newer-construction Aliana and Veranda lots, Tifway 419 or TifTuf Bermuda. Premium Zeon Zoysia on pool-and-patio installs.'),
				array('question'=>'Will Aliana, Long Meadow Farms, or Veranda approve my re-sod?','answer'=>'Yes — provided the grass variety, install method, and timing match each community\'s architectural standards. We ship a pre-filled HOA modification request package with every Aliana, Long Meadow Farms, and Veranda proposal so the homeowner has the paperwork ready for the review board. We have cleared review at each of these communities multiple times.'),
				array('question'=>'Do I need topsoil amendment on a Richmond install?','answer'=>'Almost always, and the amendment depth and composition will vary across the lot rather than being uniform. Aliana and Veranda lots typically need 2 inches of amendment on relatively predictable sandy loam. Pecan Grove and Greatwood lots typically need 2.5–3 inches of amendment plus pH correction due to accumulated leaf litter under mature canopy. Floodplain-adjacent lots need amendment plus drainage tie-in. We soil-test every Richmond lot before quoting amendment specs.'),
				array('question'=>'Do you do acreage installs along the FM 762 corridor?','answer'=>'Yes — FM 762 corridor acreage south of Richmond proper is one of our hybrid sod-plus-hydroseed segments. These properties almost always pencil out as hybrid plans: premium St. Augustine sod near the home, pool, and outdoor kitchen; engineered Bermuda hydroseeding across back acreage and pasture. Equestrian-property installs are common here, with grazing-safe Bermuda seed blends across turn-out zones.'),
				array('question'=>'Can you handle the drainage issues common in Richmond back yards?','answer'=>'Yes — and on most Richmond lots, drainage correction is built into the standard scope rather than an upcharge. We regrade for 2% minimum slope away from foundation as part of every install. On lots that need more (low spots that pond, easements that hold water, properties backing up to the Brazos floodway), we install French drains tied into existing storm-water infrastructure. We work directly with civil-engineering firms on the larger floodplain corrections.'),
				array('question'=>'What does a typical Richmond sod install cost?','answer'=>'Richmond sod pricing varies based on soil mapping, floodplain proximity, canopy work required, and project size. As a reference: typical Richmond residential front-yard re-sods range from $2,800 on smaller Aliana lots to $7,000+ on larger Pecan Grove and Greatwood properties. Full-yard quarter-acre installs typically run $5,500 to $13,000. Acreage hybrid plans on Brazos-adjacent or FM 762 corridor properties typically run $14,000 to $42,000+. We provide line-item pricing on every Richmond proposal.'),
