		array(
			'name' => 'Tomball', 'slug' => 'tomball-tx',
			'fields' => array(
				'short_heading' => 'Tomball',
				'heading_text' => 'Sod Installation & Grass Establishment in Tomball, TX',
				'short_description_listing_view' => 'From in-town Tomball to Rose Hill acreage, we cover the full FM 2920 corridor.',
				'short_description' => 'From in-town Tomball to Rose Hill acreage, we cover the full FM 2920 corridor.',
				'tagline' => '"Sod where appearance matters most. Hydroseeding where scale matters most."',
				'installation_done' => '290+ installed · Since 2016',
				'lawn_establishment_heading' => 'Premium sod & lawn establishment for Tomball, TX properties',
				'lawn_establishment_sub_heading' => 'Whether you\'re a homeowner upgrading the front yard, a builder finishing a custom home, or an HOA restoring entry-feature lawns — GrassHouston brings the right mix of sod, hydroseeding, and hybrid solutions to Tomball, Texas.',
				'lawn_establishment_content' => 'Tomball sits in a transition zone where the sandy loam profiles of north Houston give way to the prairie-influenced soils of northwest Harris County — and that geography drives almost every install decision we make here. Tomball lots drain faster than most of the metro, hold less moisture and nutrients in the root zone, and benefit from organic amendment that gumbo-clay lots do not need. Watering schedules during establishment are also different — shorter, more frequent cycles rather than the deeper longer cycles that work on clay-influenced lots in Pearland or Katy.

The defining Tomball factor beyond soil is the FM 2920 corridor\'s mix of suburban subdivision and rural acreage. Rose Hill, Pinecrest, Treeline, and Wildwood at Northpointe span the suburban side; once you head west on FM 2920 toward Magnolia, lot sizes jump to 1-acre custom homesites and 5-acre rural acreage tracts. This is hybrid sod-plus-hydroseed territory by default on properties over half an acre.

Tree canopy is a real factor in established Tomball neighborhoods. Mature pine and oak canopy creates the same install constraints we see in The Woodlands and Kingwood — Bermuda fails under canopy, and shade-tolerant St. Augustine is the right call on roughly 80% of established Tomball lots.

Recommended grass for Tomball: St. Augustine, Zoysia · Soil profile: Sandy soils dominate, with pockets of clay near low-lying creek areas.',
				'deep_dive_heading' => 'What makes Tomball lawn installation different',
				'deep_dive_content' => 'Tomball, Texas is the lawn-installation market where northwest Houston suburban subdivision meets rural acreage country, and the install strategy has to flex across both. The defining geographic fact is the soil profile: Tomball sits in a transition zone where the sandy loam of north Houston gives way to the prairie-influenced soils of northwest Harris County. Most established Tomball lots drain faster than the gumbo-clay lots in Pearland or the prairie-clay lots in Katy. That faster drainage is a double-edged sword — water and nutrients pass through the root zone faster, which means shorter and more frequent watering cycles during establishment, plus more attentive organic amendment to hold moisture and nutrients at the surface where new sod roots can reach them. Our 290+ Tomball installs since 2016 have given us a working map of which sub-markets drain how fast, and we calibrate the irrigation and amendment plan to each.

The defining Tomball commercial-to-residential pattern is the FM 2920 corridor running west from I-45. Closer to I-45, the corridor is suburban subdivision residential — Treeline, Pinecrest, Wildwood at Northpointe, and the older sections of in-town Tomball. As you head west on FM 2920 toward Magnolia, lot sizes scale up rapidly from suburban quarter-acre to 1-acre custom homesites in Rose Hill to true 5-acre and 10-acre rural acreage tracts. Suburban subdivision installs run on standard residential scope (front yard, around the home, drainage corrections); acreage installs west of Rose Hill almost universally fit the hybrid sod-plus-hydroseed model that built our business — premium sod near the home and entertaining zones, engineered Bermuda hydroseeding across pasture and outer-fence-line acreage.

Tomball tree canopy is the third factor that catches new homeowners off guard. Established Tomball neighborhoods sit under 30–50 years of accumulated pine and oak canopy, with the same install constraints we see in The Woodlands and Kingwood: filtered light averaging 4–5 hours per day, surface tree roots competing aggressively with new sod for water and nutrients, and pine-needle and leaf litter driving soil pH downward over time. Bermuda fails almost universally under established Tomball canopy. We install shade-tolerant St. Augustine (typically Palmetto, sometimes Sapphire for the deepest shade pockets) on roughly 80% of established Tomball residential properties, with selective canopy limb-up and surface-root pruning built into standard install scope.

Tomball also has a meaningful equestrian and FFA-property segment along the FM 2920 corridor west and the SH-249 corridor north. These properties layer additional complexity onto standard acreage work: paddock perimeters need traffic-tolerant Bermuda, turn-out zones need grazing-safe seed blends (no toxic species), and barn surrounds often need premium sod for the show-property look. We coordinate with fence contractors and barn builders on sequencing across these properties, and we have delivered enough Tomball equestrian installs to know how to plan for all of it.',
				'grass_installation_conetent' => '<p class="text-stone-600 mb-5 leading-relaxed">Sandy soils dominate, with pockets of clay near low-lying creek areas.</p>

<ul class="space-y-3 mb-6">
