			'show' => array('st_augustine'=>'1','bermuda'=>'0','zoysia'=>'1'),
			'faqs' => array(
				array('question'=>'Why does my Tomball lawn need more frequent watering than my neighbor in Katy?','answer'=>'Because Tomball soil is dramatically different from Katy soil. Tomball sits on sandy loam that drains faster than the prairie clay under Katy or the gumbo clay under Pearland. Water passes through the root zone faster, which means shorter and more frequent watering cycles during establishment (and ongoing) compared to clay-soil neighbors. Our standard Tomball care calendar reflects this — typically 2–3 shorter cycles per day during the first week vs. 1–2 longer cycles on clay-soil installs.'),
				array('question'=>'What grass works best in Tomball?','answer'=>'On established Tomball lots under mature canopy (Treeline, Pinecrest, older Rose Hill, in-town Tomball), Palmetto St. Augustine — it handles the 4–5 hours of filtered light most established Tomball lots actually get, tolerates Tomball sandy loam once installed over an organic-amended surface, and recovers from foot traffic well in family back yards. On full-sun newer Wildwood at Northpointe filings and rural acreage front yards, Tifway 419 or TifTuf Bermuda. Premium Zeon Zoysia on pool-and-patio installs.'),
				array('question'=>'Do I really need different soil amendment in Tomball than in Pearland or Katy?','answer'=>'Yes — and this is one of the most common mistakes other contractors make in Tomball. Pearland and Katy installs typically need sandy loam amendment to improve drainage on clay subgrades. Tomball installs typically need compost-heavy organic amendment to improve moisture retention on naturally fast-draining sandy loam. The amendment is different, the depth is different, and the long-term care calendar is different. We tune all of it to Tomball specifically.'),
				array('question'=>'Do you do equestrian and FFA properties along FM 2920?','answer'=>'Constantly — FM 2920 west and SH-249 corridor equestrian properties are one of our most active Tomball segments. These properties almost always pencil out as hybrid plans: premium St. Augustine sod near the home, barn surrounds, paddock perimeters, and turn-out gates; engineered Bermuda hydroseeding across pasture and outer-fence-line acreage with grazing-safe seed blends (no toxic species). We coordinate with fence contractors and barn builders on sequencing.'),
				array('question'=>'Why does my Tomball lawn keep thinning under the trees?','answer'=>'Three reasons: insufficient filtered light reaching the turf (Tomball canopy has grown over decades and your lawn has been losing light gradually), surface tree roots competing for water and nutrients on fast-draining sandy loam, and accumulated pine-needle litter driving soil pH downward. The fix is the whole package: canopy limb-up of the lower 8–10 feet, surface-root pruning, pH-corrected organic amendment, and the right shade-tolerant variety. We include all of that in our standard Tomball canopy-lot install scope.'),
				array('question'=>'Can you handle drainage issues on Tomball lots?','answer'=>'Yes — and drainage in Tomball is genuinely different from drainage in clay-soil markets. Because Tomball sandy loam drains fast, drainage issues tend to be specific low spots that pond after heavy rain rather than the general slow-drainage problems we see in Pearland or Katy. We install French drains, channel drains, or catch basins in any specific low spots, and we regrade for 2% minimum slope away from the foundation as part of every install.'),
				array('question'=>'How long does a Tomball install take to complete?','answer'=>'Most Tomball residential installs complete in 1–3 working days on quarter-acre to half-acre lots. Heavy-canopy installs in Treeline and Pinecrest can add 1–2 days for canopy limb-up and root pruning. Acreage hybrid projects in Rose Hill and along FM 2920 west typically take 5–10 working days depending on prep and the sod-to-hydroseed ratio.'),
				array('question'=>'What does a typical Tomball sod install cost?','answer'=>'Tomball sod pricing varies based on lot size, canopy work required, organic amendment specs, grass variety, and whether the property is suburban or acreage. As a reference: typical Tomball front-yard re-sods on Treeline, Pinecrest, and Wildwood at Northpointe lots range from $2,800 to $6,500 depending on canopy work. Full-yard quarter-acre suburban installs typically run $5,000 to $11,000. Acreage hybrid plans on 1-acre to 5-acre Rose Hill properties typically run $13,000 to $40,000+. We provide line-item pricing on every Tomball proposal.'),
			),
