				'installation_done' => '210+ installed · Since 2018',
				'lawn_establishment_heading' => 'Premium sod & lawn establishment for Magnolia, TX properties',
				'lawn_establishment_sub_heading' => 'Whether you\'re a homeowner upgrading the front yard, a builder finishing a custom home, or an HOA restoring entry-feature lawns — GrassHouston brings the right mix of sod, hydroseeding, and hybrid solutions to Magnolia, Texas.',
				'lawn_establishment_content' => 'Magnolia is acreage country northwest of Houston with sandy loam soils, mature pine and hardwood canopy on many lots, and a steady stream of custom-home builds along FM 1488, FM 1774, and the SH-249 corridor. Hybrid sod-plus-hydroseed plans are the default rather than the exception.

High Meadow Ranch, Mostyn Manor, Magnolia Ridge, and Westwood are the residential anchors; once you move out to 5-acre and 10-acre tracts, hybrid-turf becomes the only economically viable approach — premium sod near the home, engineered hydroseed across outer acreage.

Tree cover in established Magnolia neighborhoods drives variety selection — Palmetto St. Augustine on canopy lots, Bermuda on full-sun acreage. We zone every Magnolia install by canopy density and project size.

Recommended grass for Magnolia: St. Augustine, Zoysia · Soil profile: Sandy loam with shaded tree-covered lots — St. Augustine performs well.',
				'deep_dive_heading' => 'What makes Magnolia lawn installation different',
				'deep_dive_content' => 'Magnolia, Texas occupies one of the most acreage-focused lawn-installation markets in our service area, with sandy loam soils, mature pine and hardwood canopy on established lots, and a custom-build segment along FM 1488 and FM 1774 that drives a consistent stream of new-construction work. Roughly 210 of our installs since 2018 have been in Magnolia, and the segment mix skews heavily toward 1-acre+ properties with a meaningful subset of 5-acre to 20+ acre estate and equestrian tracts. Hybrid sod-plus-hydroseed plans are the default rather than the exception.

The defining Magnolia environmental factor is the sandy loam soil under canopy. Magnolia sits on sandy loam that drains quickly — similar to Willis and Conroe to the east — but layered with mature pine and hardwood canopy on most established lots that drives soil pH downward over time and reduces filtered light to 4–5 hours per day. Our standard established-Magnolia install combines compost-heavy organic amendment (for sandy loam moisture retention), pH correction with lime amendment (for canopy acid drift), and Palmetto St. Augustine variety selection (for shade tolerance).

Acreage and hybrid-turf work is the second defining Magnolia segment. Most Magnolia lots over half an acre pencil out as hybrid plans by default — premium St. Augustine or Zoysia sod near the home and pool, engineered Bermuda hydroseeding across outer acreage and pasture. Equestrian properties get grazing-safe seed blends, and we coordinate with fence contractors and barn builders on installs that integrate with new fencing or barn construction.

Custom-build work is the third defining Magnolia segment. FM 1488 and FM 1774 corridor custom builds, High Meadow Ranch estate construction, and Mostyn Manor newer custom filings drive consistent new-construction work. We coordinate with Magnolia-area custom builders, landscape architects, irrigation contractors, and pool companies on final-grade installs that have to integrate with the rest of the new-construction trades.',
				'grass_installation_conetent' => '<p class="text-stone-600 mb-5 leading-relaxed">Sandy loam with shaded tree-covered lots — St. Augustine performs well.</p>

