			'show' => array('st_augustine'=>'1','bermuda'=>'1','zoysia'=>'0'),
			'faqs' => array(
				array('question'=>'Why is drainage such a big deal on every League City install?','answer'=>'Because League City sits on heavy coastal clay over a chronically high water table that sits within 12–18 inches of the surface across most of the city. Even on lots that drain reasonably at the surface, the bottom 2–3 inches of any topsoil layer stays saturated through most of the rainy season. Sod roots that grow into that saturated zone rot from the bottom up, and the lawn fails — not from the visible top but from the invisible bottom. Every successful League City install starts with a drainage solution before we discuss grass varieties.'),
				array('question'=>'Why did my last lawn keep dying within 18 months?','answer'=>'Almost certainly drainage. If the contractor laid sod directly on top of saturated coastal clay without addressing the water table — no elevation fill, no French drain installation, no surface regrading — the sod looked great on closing day and rotted from the bottom up over the next 12–18 months. We see this pattern dozens of times per year in League City. The fix is not a different grass variety; it is a real drainage plan that gives roots a non-saturated zone to grow into.'),
				array('question'=>'What grass works best in League City?','answer'=>'Palmetto St. Augustine on most lots — it is meaningfully more water-tolerant than Bermuda on the saturated subsoil that defines League City coastal clay, and it clears HOA architectural review at Tuscan Lakes, Mar Bella, and Westover Park without friction. On full-sun lots with corrected drainage we sometimes install Tifway 419 Bermuda. Premium Zeon Zoysia on pool-and-patio installs where the drainage compliance is in place.'),
				array('question'=>'Do you handle storm and hurricane flood recovery?','answer'=>'Yes — and it is a meaningful recurring part of our League City book. After every named storm season (Ike, Harvey, Imelda, Beryl, and every storm in between), we restore League City lawns with drainage redesign, elevation corrections, and water-tolerant variety selection. We coordinate with insurance adjusters on documented loss claims, and we recommend long-term planning rather than just the cheapest re-sod that perpetuates the failure cycle.'),
				array('question'=>'Will Tuscan Lakes, Mar Bella, or Magnolia Creek approve my re-sod?','answer'=>'Yes — provided the grass variety, install method, and timing match each community\'s architectural standards. We ship a pre-filled HOA modification request package with every Tuscan Lakes, Mar Bella, Westover Park, and Magnolia Creek proposal so the homeowner has the paperwork ready for the review board. We have cleared review at each of these communities multiple times.'),
				array('question'=>'Do you handle French drain installation and elevation fill?','answer'=>'Yes — and on lower-elevation League City lots, both are built into the standard install scope rather than treated as upcharges. We install French drains tied into existing storm-water infrastructure on lots that hold water, and we add fill to bring the finished sod surface at least 4 inches above the historical high-water-table line. We coordinate with civil-engineering firms on larger drainage redesigns where required.'),
				array('question'=>'How long does a League City install take to complete?','answer'=>'Most League City residential installs complete in 2–4 working days on quarter-acre to half-acre lots because of the drainage component — French drain installation adds 1 day, elevation fill adds another. Standard residential installs without major drainage work complete in 1–2 days. Multi-event storm recovery installs with drainage redesign typically take 5–10 working days depending on scope.'),
				array('question'=>'What does a typical League City sod install cost?','answer'=>'League City sod pricing varies enormously based on drainage scope. As a reference: typical League City front-yard re-sods on Tuscan Lakes, Mar Bella, and Westover Park lots range from $3,500 to $7,500 depending on drainage scope. Lots requiring French drain installation and elevation fill typically run $8,000 to $15,000 for the full install. Full-yard quarter-acre installs typically run $6,500 to $15,000+. Storm recovery installs with drainage redesign typically run $12,000 to $30,000+ but break the repeat-failure cycle. Line-item pricing on every proposal.'),
			),
