		array(
			'name' => 'Friendswood', 'slug' => 'friendswood-tx',
			'fields' => array(
				'short_heading' => 'Friendswood',
				'heading_text' => 'Sod Installation & Grass Establishment in Friendswood, TX',
				'short_description_listing_view' => 'Established neighborhoods with mature tree cover demand a careful grass selection — we know Friendswood.',
				'short_description' => 'Established neighborhoods with mature tree cover demand a careful grass selection — we know Friendswood.',
				'tagline' => '"Sod where appearance matters most. Hydroseeding where scale matters most."',
				'installation_done' => '220+ installed · Since 2017',
				'lawn_establishment_heading' => 'Premium sod & lawn establishment for Friendswood, TX properties',
				'lawn_establishment_sub_heading' => 'Whether you\'re a homeowner upgrading the front yard, a builder finishing a custom home, or an HOA restoring entry-feature lawns — GrassHouston brings the right mix of sod, hydroseeding, and hybrid solutions to Friendswood, Texas.',
				'lawn_establishment_content' => 'Friendswood sits between Houston and League City on coastal-clay subgrades with the same drainage challenges as the broader I-45 South corridor, layered with mature canopy in established Sterling Creek, Forest Bend, West Ranch, and Autumn Creek filings. Drainage and shade-tolerance are the entire conversation on most Friendswood installs.

Mature canopy in older Friendswood neighborhoods creates installation constraints we plan for explicitly — filtered light, surface tree roots competing with sod, and pH-shifted soil from years of leaf litter. Palmetto St. Augustine dominates here; Bermuda fails reliably under Friendswood canopy.

Clear Creek-adjacent flood exposure adds a third factor on lower-elevation Friendswood lots. We coordinate elevation review and French drain installation on flood-prone properties before recommending grass varieties.

Recommended grass for Friendswood: St. Augustine · Soil profile: Clay-heavy with mature canopy in older sections — St. Augustine dominates here.',
				'deep_dive_heading' => 'What makes Friendswood lawn installation different',
				'deep_dive_content' => 'Friendswood, Texas occupies a distinct lawn-installation market between the Houston metro proper and the League City coastal corridor. The defining geographic facts are coastal-clay subgrades that share characteristics with neighboring League City and Pearland, mature established-residential canopy across Sterling Creek, Forest Bend, West Ranch, and Autumn Creek that creates filtered-light install constraints, and Clear Creek-adjacent flood exposure on a meaningful subset of Friendswood lots. Roughly 220 of our installs since 2017 have been in Friendswood, and the patterns repeat consistently enough that we now design every Friendswood quote around the specific combination of canopy density, soil profile, and flood exposure on the lot.

The defining Friendswood residential factor is the mature canopy in established neighborhoods. 40+ years of accumulated oak and pecan canopy across Sterling Creek, Forest Bend, and the older West Ranch filings create the same install constraints we see further north in The Woodlands and Spring — filtered light averaging 4–5 hours per day, surface tree roots competing with new sod for water and nutrients, and accumulated leaf litter driving soil pH downward over time. Bermuda fails almost universally under established Friendswood canopy. Palmetto St. Augustine dominates roughly 90% of established Friendswood residential installs, with canopy limb-up, surface-root pruning, and pH-corrected amendment built into standard scope.

Coastal-clay drainage is the second defining factor and it overlaps with the canopy-heavy older sections of town. Friendswood sits on heavy clay similar to League City and Pearland, and the combination of dense canopy plus poor-draining clay creates drainage micro-issues on many lots — water pooling in shaded zones where evaporation is slowest, root rot in saturated low spots, and lawn failure not from canopy alone but from canopy plus standing water. Our standard Friendswood install corrects drainage in tandem with canopy work, and we install French drains tied into existing storm-water infrastructure on the lots that need them.

Clear Creek-adjacent flood exposure is the third factor. Lower-elevation Friendswood properties backing up to Clear Creek or its tributaries (particularly older Forest Bend filings) flooded during Harvey, Imelda, and recent storm events. We approach these recovery installs with the same drainage-first discipline we apply in League City — elevation review, French drain tie-in, and water-tolerant variety selection on top of standard Friendswood canopy and amendment work.',
