			'faqs' => array(
				array('question'=>'Why does my Friendswood lawn keep thinning out under my trees?','answer'=>'Because mature Friendswood canopy (oak, pecan, magnolia) reduces filtered light to 4–5 hours per day on most established lots, surface tree feeder roots compete aggressively with grass roots, and decades of accumulated leaf litter have driven soil pH downward. The fix is the whole package: canopy limb-up of the lower 8–10 feet, surface-root pruning, pH-corrected amendment, and shade-tolerant Palmetto St. Augustine. We include all of that in standard Friendswood canopy-lot scope.'),
				array('question'=>'What grass works best in Friendswood?','answer'=>'On established Friendswood lots under mature canopy (Sterling Creek, Forest Bend, older West Ranch, Stevenson Park area), Palmetto St. Augustine — it handles the filtered light, tolerates Friendswood coastal-clay soil over an amended surface, and clears any applicable HOA review. On full-sun newer-construction Autumn Creek and adjacent filings with corrected drainage, Tifway 419 or TifTuf Bermuda. Premium Zeon Zoysia on pool-and-patio installs.'),
				array('question'=>'Do you handle Clear Creek flood recovery in Forest Bend?','answer'=>'Yes — Forest Bend and other Clear Creek-adjacent Friendswood properties that flooded during Harvey, Imelda, or recent events are a meaningful recurring part of our Friendswood book. We restore these lawns with drainage redesign, elevation review, French drain installation tied into storm-water infrastructure, and water-tolerant Palmetto St. Augustine selection. We coordinate with insurance adjusters on documented claims.'),
				array('question'=>'Why does Friendswood need both canopy work AND coastal-clay drainage corrections?','answer'=>'Because the combination is what makes Friendswood installs uniquely demanding. Canopy alone (without drainage corrections) creates shaded saturated zones where root rot fails the lawn. Drainage corrections alone (without canopy work) leave the sod struggling for light and competing with surface tree roots. We do both as part of standard scope on every Friendswood install with mature canopy.'),
				array('question'=>'Will my HOA approve my Friendswood re-sod?','answer'=>'Yes — Friendswood-area HOAs (Sterling Creek, West Ranch, Autumn Creek, and others) all enforce architectural standards and require modification request paperwork for turf replacement. We ship a pre-filled HOA modification request package with every Friendswood proposal where review applies and have cleared review at each major community multiple times.'),
				array('question'=>'Do I need pH amendment on a Friendswood install?','answer'=>'On most established Friendswood lots, yes. Decades of oak and pecan leaf litter under mature canopy have driven soil pH below the 6.0–7.0 range St. Augustine prefers. We measure pH on every Friendswood install and add lime where the test indicates it. Without pH correction, even Palmetto St. Augustine struggles within two seasons in Friendswood canopy zones.'),
				array('question'=>'How long does a Friendswood install take to complete?','answer'=>'Most Friendswood residential installs complete in 2–4 working days on quarter-acre to half-acre lots because of the canopy and drainage components — canopy limb-up adds 1 day, French drain installation adds another. Standard installs without major canopy or drainage work complete in 1–2 days. Multi-event flood-recovery installs with drainage redesign typically take 5–10 working days.'),
				array('question'=>'What does a typical Friendswood sod install cost?','answer'=>'Friendswood sod pricing reflects the standard scope of canopy work, surface-root pruning, pH-corrected amendment, and coastal-clay drainage corrections. As a reference: typical Friendswood front-yard re-sods range from $3,500 to $7,500. Full-yard quarter-acre installs typically run $6,500 to $14,000. Pool-and-patio installs typically run $8,500 to $20,000. Flood-recovery installs with drainage redesign typically run $10,000 to $25,000+.'),
