			'faqs' => array(
				array('question'=>'Why does Humble soil vary so much across a single property?','answer'=>'Because Humble sits in a transitional soil zone northeast of Houston where sandy loam pockets transition to clay-influenced soil, particularly closer to Lake Houston. A single Humble lot can have meaningfully different soil zones across the property. We dig 3–5 soil probes on every Humble install and calibrate amendment to each zone rather than running a generic spec.'),
				array('question'=>'What grass works best in Humble?','answer'=>'On established Humble lots under mature canopy (Atascocita, Eagle Springs, older Walden filings), Palmetto St. Augustine — it handles the 4–5 hours of filtered light most established Humble lots actually get, tolerates Humble transitional soil over a calibrated amendment, and clears HOA architectural review at all major Humble communities. On full-sun newer Fall Creek filings, Tifway 419 or TifTuf Bermuda. Premium Zeon Zoysia on pool-and-patio installs.'),
				array('question'=>'Will Atascocita, Eagle Springs, Fall Creek, or Walden approve my re-sod?','answer'=>'Yes — provided the grass variety, install method, and timing match each community\'s architectural standards. We ship a pre-filled HOA modification request package with every Atascocita, Eagle Springs, Fall Creek, and Walden on Lake Houston proposal. We have cleared review at each of these communities multiple times.'),
				array('question'=>'Do you handle Lake Houston-adjacent shoreline work in Walden?','answer'=>'Yes — Walden on Lake Houston and other lake-fronting Humble estate properties often require shoreline coordination, slope stabilization, retaining-wall-adjacent turf installation, and elevation review on top of standard residential install scope. We coordinate with our erosion-control crews and with dock builders on lake-fronting work.'),
				array('question'=>'Why does my Atascocita lawn keep thinning under the trees?','answer'=>'Three reasons typical for established Atascocita canopy lots: insufficient filtered light (decades of canopy maturity have steadily reduced light reaching the lawn), surface pine and oak feeder roots competing for water and nutrients, and accumulated pine-needle litter driving soil pH downward. The fix is canopy limb-up of the lower 8–10 feet, surface-root pruning, pH-corrected organic amendment, and Palmetto St. Augustine. We include all of that in standard Atascocita scope.'),
				array('question'=>'Do you do builder closeout work in Fall Creek and newer Humble filings?','answer'=>'Yes — multi-lot final-grade sod installs for production and custom builders across Fall Creek, newer Atascocita filings, and adjacent Humble subdivisions are a meaningful part of our Humble book. We schedule against the builder\'s closing calendar, coordinate with the GC superintendent on staging, and ship submittal packages that match the bid documents.'),
				array('question'=>'How long does a Humble install take to complete?','answer'=>'Most Humble residential installs complete in 1–3 working days on quarter-acre to half-acre lots. Heavy-canopy installs in Atascocita and Eagle Springs can add 1–2 days for canopy limb-up and root pruning. Lakeside Walden installs with shoreline coordination typically take 5–10 working days depending on scope. Multi-zone soil amendment adds 1 day on properties with significant zone variability.'),
				array('question'=>'What does a typical Humble sod install cost?','answer'=>'Humble sod pricing varies based on sub-market, canopy work required, soil-zone variability, and lake proximity. As a reference: typical Humble front-yard re-sods on Atascocita, Eagle Springs, Fall Creek, and Walden lots range from $3,000 to $7,000. Full-yard quarter-acre installs typically run $5,500 to $13,500. Premium Lake Houston-adjacent installs with shoreline coordination typically run $10,000 to $25,000+. We provide line-item pricing on every Humble proposal.'),
