		array(
			'name' => 'Kingwood', 'slug' => 'kingwood-tx',
			'fields' => array(
				'short_heading' => 'Kingwood',
				'heading_text' => 'Sod Installation & Grass Establishment in Kingwood, TX',
				'short_description_listing_view' => 'The Livable Forest demands a shade-tolerant install plan — Kingwood is St. Augustine country.',
				'short_description' => 'The Livable Forest demands a shade-tolerant install plan — Kingwood is St. Augustine country.',
				'tagline' => '"Sod where appearance matters most. Hydroseeding where scale matters most."',
				'installation_done' => '240+ installed · Since 2017',
				'lawn_establishment_heading' => 'Premium sod & lawn establishment for Kingwood, TX properties',
				'lawn_establishment_sub_heading' => 'Whether you\'re a homeowner upgrading the front yard, a builder finishing a custom home, or an HOA restoring entry-feature lawns — GrassHouston brings the right mix of sod, hydroseeding, and hybrid solutions to Kingwood, Texas.',
				'lawn_establishment_content' => 'Kingwood markets itself as the Livable Forest for good reason — almost every lot sits under a heavy pine and hardwood canopy. That canopy is gorgeous, and it is also the single biggest constraint on what grass will actually thrive on a Kingwood property. Bermuda fails here almost universally; we install St. Augustine on roughly 95% of Kingwood lawns we touch, with Palmetto or Sapphire varieties for the most heavily shaded back yards.

Kingwood soil is sandy loam with naturally low pH from decades of pine-needle drop. That acidic surface layer matters: we soil-test before every install, amend the pH where needed, and selectively limb up the lower 8–10 feet of tree canopy to give the new sod a fighting chance at the light it needs. Without that step, even shade-tolerant St. Augustine struggles within two seasons.

Flood recovery is the other Kingwood reality. We have re-sodded and restored lawns across Kings Forest, Forest Cove, Kings River, Bear Branch, and Kingwood Greens after multiple flooding events, and we know how to assess whether a lawn is genuinely salvageable or whether full replacement (with corrected drainage) is the only honest answer.

Recommended grass for Kingwood: St. Augustine · Soil profile: Sandy loam under heavy pine canopy — shade tolerance drives grass selection.',
				'deep_dive_heading' => 'What makes Kingwood lawn installation different',
				'deep_dive_content' => 'Kingwood, Texas is a lawn-installation market defined almost entirely by its mature tree canopy. The community was developed in the early 1970s as \'The Livable Forest,\' and 50+ years of growth have created a canopy so dense across Kings Forest, Forest Cove, Kings River, Bear Branch, and Kingwood Greens that filtered light averages 3–4 hours per day on many established lots. That canopy is gorgeous and it defines Kingwood — but it is also the single defining install constraint on every Kingwood lawn we touch. Roughly 240 of our installs since 2017 have been in Kingwood, and the shade-tolerance, pH amendment, and canopy management work is the entire conversation.

The defining Kingwood environmental factor is canopy pH drift. Decades of pine-needle and hardwood leaf litter under most Kingwood lots have driven soil pH well below the 6.0–7.0 range that St. Augustine prefers. We routinely measure pH below 5.5 on established Kingwood installs. Acidic pH locks up nutrients and prevents healthy root development even when amendment and irrigation are otherwise correct. We soil-test every Kingwood install and add lime to correct pH where the test indicates it. This step is non-negotiable on most established Kingwood lots, and it is the step contractors most commonly skip on the way to a failed install.

The Kingwood flood-recovery segment is the third defining factor. Kings Forest, Forest Cove, and the West Fork-adjacent neighborhoods all flooded heavily during Harvey, Imelda, and the regular rain events that have hit the metro since 2017. Our Kingwood book has a meaningful recurring flood-recovery component, and we always assess whether the underlying drainage failure has been corrected before recommending a re-sod. Some Kingwood lots have flooded multiple times because the original lot drainage was never adequate; we coordinate with civil-engineering firms on elevation review and French drain installation where required.

Kingwood-area HOA architectural review is the fourth defining factor that catches new homeowners off guard. The Kingwood Service Association and the various village HOAs across Kings Forest, Forest Cove, Kings River, Bear Branch, and Kingwood Greens all enforce specific grass-variety standards and require modification request paperwork for any turf replacement. We have cleared review at each of these villages multiple times and ship a pre-filled modification request package with every Kingwood proposal.',
