				'tagline' => '"Sod where appearance matters most. Hydroseeding where scale matters most."',
				'installation_done' => '210+ installed · Since 2017',
				'lawn_establishment_heading' => 'Premium sod & lawn establishment for Missouri City, TX properties',
				'lawn_establishment_sub_heading' => 'Whether you\'re a homeowner upgrading the front yard, a builder finishing a custom home, or an HOA restoring entry-feature lawns — GrassHouston brings the right mix of sod, hydroseeding, and hybrid solutions to Missouri City, Texas.',
				'lawn_establishment_content' => 'Missouri City sits on some of the most expansive, active swelling clay soil in the southwest Houston metro — denser than typical Houston gumbo and more aggressive in its swell-and-shrink cycle than even Pearland clay. Sod laid directly on raw Missouri City clay will fail within 18–24 months. Soil amendment and drainage are the entire conversation here.

Sienna, Riverstone, First Colony, and Quail Valley all share these expansive-clay patterns layered on top of strict architectural-review standards. Sienna especially enforces grass-variety standards rigorously; we ship the HOA modification request package with every Sienna proposal and have cleared review there many times.

Missouri City golf-community work along Quail Valley Golf Course adds canopy and elevation variation to the mix. We zone every Missouri City install by canopy density and HOA standards, and we calibrate clay amendment to each lot.

Recommended grass for Missouri City: St. Augustine, Bermuda · Soil profile: Clay-heavy, with active swelling soils — installation depth and prep matter.',
				'deep_dive_heading' => 'What makes Missouri City lawn installation different',
				'deep_dive_content' => 'Missouri City, Texas occupies a distinct lawn-installation market within the southwest Houston metro because of its combination of dramatically expansive clay soil and strict master-planned-community architectural review across Sienna, Riverstone, First Colony, and Quail Valley. Our 210+ Missouri City installs since 2017 have given us a working map of the expansive-clay patterns here, which are denser and more aggressive than typical Houston gumbo. Every Missouri City install we deliver starts with a deep clay soil test and a non-negotiable 2–3 inch sandy loam amendment scope.

The defining Missouri City environmental factor is the active swelling clay. Where Houston gumbo is bad and Pearland clay is worse, Missouri City clay is comparable to Pearland in density but adds heavier organic deposit from prior agricultural use in some sub-markets — which creates additional drainage variability lot-to-lot. Sod laid directly on raw Missouri City clay cannot push roots into the surface; roots bounce off and the lawn fails within 18–24 months. Our standard Missouri City install includes the 2–3 inch sandy loam topsoil amendment, drainage corrections to pull water away from the foundation, and Palmetto St. Augustine variety selection on the partial-canopy lots that dominate established Missouri City.

Sienna architectural review is the second defining Missouri City factor. Sienna enforces specific grass-variety standards (Palmetto St. Augustine is the most common approved variety on visible front and side yards), requires modification request paperwork for any turf replacement, and runs an active review process that has teeth. We have cleared Sienna review many times and ship a pre-filled modification request package with every Sienna proposal. The same applies on Riverstone, First Colony, and Quail Valley — each community has its own architectural standards, and we are familiar with each.

Quail Valley golf-community work is the third defining Missouri City segment. Quail Valley Golf Course and its surrounding estate residential add canopy and elevation variation to the standard Missouri City clay-amendment scope. Some Quail Valley lots have mature pecan and oak canopy that requires pre-install canopy work, surface-root pruning, and pH amendment in addition to the standard expansive-clay amendment. We zone every Quail Valley install by canopy density and HOA standards.',
