				'lawn_establishment_sub_heading' => 'Whether you\'re a homeowner upgrading the front yard, a builder finishing a custom home, or an HOA restoring entry-feature lawns — GrassHouston brings the right mix of sod, hydroseeding, and hybrid solutions to Clear Lake, Texas.',
				'lawn_establishment_content' => 'Clear Lake lawns live with two conditions homeowners always underestimate: a chronically high water table from Galveston Bay proximity, and salt-influenced soil that quietly kills grass varieties that work fine 20 miles inland. Drainage and salt tolerance are the entire conversation here, before we ever talk about appearance.

El Lago, Nassau Bay, Bay Oaks, and University Park all share these coastal patterns layered on top of NASA-area lot constraints — older, established neighborhoods with mature canopy in some pockets, full-sun newer filings in others, and storm-surge-zone elevations across most of the metro. Every Clear Lake install starts with an elevation analysis and a water-tolerance review of the proposed grass variety.

Hurricane and tropical-storm recovery is the third defining Clear Lake reality. Ike, Harvey, Imelda, and Beryl all hit this market hard. Our Clear Lake book has a meaningful recurring recovery component, and we always quote recovery installs with the underlying drainage failure addressed — not just a re-sod over the same broken setup.

Recommended grass for Clear Lake: St. Augustine · Soil profile: High water table and salt-influenced soils near the bay.',
				'deep_dive_heading' => 'What makes Clear Lake lawn installation different',
				'deep_dive_content' => 'Clear Lake, Texas is the most coast-influenced lawn installation market in our Greater Houston service area, and the install constraints are genuinely different from any market more than 20 miles inland. The combination of Galveston Bay proximity (with the corresponding storm-surge elevation profile across most of the metro), a chronically high water table that sits within 12–18 inches of the surface across most filings, and the salt influence that bay-adjacent winds carry inland creates an install environment where standard Houston-suburban specs simply do not work. Roughly 180 of our installs since 2018 have been in Clear Lake, and every single one of them has started with a water-and-salt assessment before we discussed grass varieties.

The defining Clear Lake factor is salt influence. Bay-adjacent wind, rain that carries salt off the bay during storm events, and salt-influenced soil chemistry from the underlying coastal-plain geology together mean grass varieties that thrive 30 miles inland thin out and fail in Clear Lake within two seasons. Palmetto St. Augustine is the dominant Clear Lake variety because it tolerates the combination of high water table, salt influence, and partial canopy that most Clear Lake lots present. We install Bermuda only on a small minority of Clear Lake lots — typically newer full-sun construction with corrected drainage and limited bay-wind exposure. Zoysia is rare here because the salt and water-table combination challenges Zoysia varieties more than St. Augustine.

El Lago, Nassau Bay, and the older NASA-corridor neighborhoods have their own canopy profiles — 40+ years of established live oak and pine canopy that creates the same install constraints we see further north in Spring or Tomball: filtered light averaging 4–5 hours per day, surface tree roots competing with new sod, and accumulated leaf litter shifting soil pH. Bay Oaks and University Park have mixed canopy. We zone every Clear Lake install variety selection by canopy density on the specific lot and salt-tolerance need across the property.

Hurricane and tropical-storm recovery is the third defining Clear Lake reality. The city sits in the most direct hurricane-track corridor for storms moving inland from Galveston Bay, and every named storm season produces flood-damaged, wind-damaged, and salt-damaged lawns across the metro. Our Clear Lake book includes a meaningful recurring recovery component — properties we have re-sodded after Ike, Harvey, Imelda, Beryl, and every storm in between. Recovery installs require drainage redesign, salt-flush amendment of contaminated soil, elevation review, and water-tolerant variety selection. We do not just re-sod over a property that flooded and call it done; we address what caused the original failure.',
