			'faqs' => array(
				array('question'=>'Why does Clear Lake need special grass-variety consideration?','answer'=>'Because Clear Lake sits adjacent to Galveston Bay with salt-influenced wind, rain, and soil chemistry that grass varieties thriving 30 miles inland cannot handle. Palmetto St. Augustine tolerates the combination of salt influence, high water table, and partial canopy that defines most Clear Lake lots. We install Bermuda only on a small minority of full-sun corrected-drainage lots; Zoysia is rare here because the salt-and-water-table combination challenges it more than St. Augustine.'),
				array('question'=>'Why is drainage such a big deal on every Clear Lake install?','answer'=>'Because Clear Lake sits on a chronically high water table within 12–18 inches of the surface across most filings, plus storm-surge elevation concerns from Galveston Bay proximity. Sod roots that grow into saturated subsoil rot from the bottom up. Every successful Clear Lake install starts with an elevation analysis and drainage solution before we discuss grass varieties — French drain installation, elevation fill where needed, and surface regrading away from the foundation.'),
				array('question'=>'Do you handle hurricane and storm flood recovery in El Lago, Nassau Bay, and Bay Oaks?','answer'=>'Yes — and it is a meaningful recurring part of our Clear Lake book. After every named storm season (Ike, Harvey, Imelda, Beryl, and every storm in between), we restore Clear Lake lawns with drainage redesign, elevation corrections, salt-flush soil amendment where bay water contaminated the lot, and salt-tolerant variety selection. We coordinate with insurance adjusters on documented claims.'),
				array('question'=>'What grass works best in Clear Lake?','answer'=>'Palmetto St. Augustine on roughly 90% of Clear Lake lots — it tolerates the salt influence, high water table, and partial canopy that define this market, and it clears HOA architectural review at El Lago, Nassau Bay, Bay Oaks, and University Park. On full-sun lots with corrected drainage and limited bay-wind exposure we occasionally install Tifway 419 Bermuda. Premium Zeon Zoysia is rare here because of the salt-and-water-table combination.'),
				array('question'=>'Will El Lago, Nassau Bay, Bay Oaks, or University Park approve my re-sod?','answer'=>'Yes — provided the grass variety, install method, and timing match each community\'s architectural standards. We ship a pre-filled HOA modification request package with every El Lago, Nassau Bay, Bay Oaks, and University Park proposal. We have cleared review at each of these communities multiple times.'),
				array('question'=>'Does salt influence really affect my Clear Lake lawn?','answer'=>'Yes — and it is the factor most often missed by contractors who do not specialize in coastal markets. Bay-adjacent wind carries salt inland. Storm-event rain carries salt off the bay. Underlying soil chemistry has salt influence from the coastal-plain geology. Grass varieties that thrive in Katy or Spring fail in Clear Lake within two seasons because they cannot tolerate the cumulative salt load. We select varieties with documented salt tolerance and amend soil where storm events have contaminated it.'),
				array('question'=>'How long does a Clear Lake install take to complete?','answer'=>'Most Clear Lake residential installs complete in 2–4 working days on quarter-acre to half-acre lots because of the drainage and elevation components — French drain installation adds 1 day, elevation fill adds another. Standard installs without major drainage work complete in 1–2 days. Storm recovery installs with drainage redesign and salt-flush amendment typically take 5–10 working days.'),
				array('question'=>'What does a typical Clear Lake sod install cost?','answer'=>'Clear Lake sod pricing varies based on drainage scope, salt-tolerance considerations, canopy work, and storm-recovery history. As a reference: typical Clear Lake front-yard re-sods on El Lago, Nassau Bay, Bay Oaks, and University Park lots range from $3,200 to $7,000. Lots requiring French drain installation and elevation fill typically run $7,500 to $14,000. Full-yard quarter-acre installs typically run $6,000 to $14,000. Storm recovery installs with drainage redesign typically run $10,000 to $25,000+.'),
			),
