			'show' => array('st_augustine'=>'1','bermuda'=>'1','zoysia'=>'0'),
			'faqs' => array(
				array('question'=>'Why does every Baytown lawn need aggressive decompaction?','answer'=>'Because Baytown sits on heavy coastal clay that has been compressed by decades of heavy-equipment traffic, periodic flooding, and ongoing construction along the refinery and industrial corridors. The compaction layer is dramatically denser than typical Houston gumbo. Sod laid directly on raw compacted Baytown clay cannot push roots into the surface — they bounce off the impermeable compaction layer and the lawn fails within 18–24 months. Every successful Baytown install starts with aggressive till-out of the compaction layer to 4–6 inches, followed by a 2–3 inch sandy loam amendment that gives new sod a friable rooting zone to establish in.'),
				array('question'=>'Why does new builder sod keep dying in Eagle Pointe and Brunson Estates?','answer'=>'Because the production builder lays Raleigh St. Augustine directly over raw decades-compacted Baytown clay with no amendment layer and no aggressive decompaction. It looks great on closing day and fails over the next 18–24 months as roots cannot penetrate the dense clay below, especially through Gulf Coast humidity cycles and periodic flooding events. We correct this exact pattern dozens of times per year in Eagle Pointe, Goose Creek, and Brunson Estates — strip, aggressive till-out, 2–3 inch sandy loam amendment, regrade for drainage, and reinstall with Palmetto St. Augustine.'),
				array('question'=>'What grass works best in Baytown?','answer'=>'On established Baytown residential lots with partial canopy and coastal-clay subgrades (most Eagle Pointe and Goose Creek installs), Palmetto St. Augustine — it handles the partial filtered light, tolerates the coastal-clay water profile once installed over a properly decompacted and amended surface, and clears Baytown-area HOA architectural review. On full-sun newer-construction Brunson Estates and SH-146 corridor front yards with corrected drainage and amendment, we install Tifway 419 or TifTuf Bermuda. Premium Zeon Zoysia on pool-and-patio installs where the prep is done right.'),
				array('question'=>'Do you handle hurricane and storm flood recovery in Baytown?','answer'=>'Yes — and it is a meaningful recurring part of our Baytown book. After every named storm season (Ike, Harvey, Imelda, Beryl, and every storm in between), we restore Baytown lawns with drainage redesign, elevation corrections on FEMA-mapped floodplain lots, salt-flush soil amendment on bay-water-contaminated properties, and water-tolerant Palmetto St. Augustine variety selection. We coordinate with insurance adjusters on documented loss claims and recommend long-term planning rather than the cheapest re-sod that perpetuates the failure cycle.'),
				array('question'=>'Are there industrial-corridor or refinery-adjacent soil-quality considerations I should know about?','answer'=>'On most Baytown residential lots, no — standard aggressive decompaction and amendment scope handles everything. On the rare lots where soil testing surfaces contamination concerns (typically properties very close to past industrial activity or refinery-adjacent commercial campuses), we recommend additional remediation including soil replacement rather than amendment, and quote that work transparently as a separate line item with documentation. We soil-test every Baytown lot before quoting so the homeowner knows exactly what they are dealing with.'),
				array('question'=>'Do you do refinery-adjacent commercial and industrial work along SH-146 and I-10 East?','answer'=>'Yes — Baytown industrial and refinery-adjacent commercial work is one of our most active commercial segments. We deliver hybrid sod-plus-hydroseed installs along SH-146 retail corridors, TXDOT-spec hydroseed blends on Chambers County industrial pads, bonded fiber matrix erosion control on permitted construction sites, and full SWPPP-compliance coordination with civil-engineering teams. We carry refinery-adjacent insurance coverage and ship full bid-document submittal packages with every commercial Baytown proposal.'),
				array('question'=>'Will Eagle Pointe, Brunson Estates, or my Baytown HOA approve my re-sod?','answer'=>'Yes — provided the grass variety, install method, and timing match each community\'s architectural standards. We ship a pre-filled HOA modification request package with every Eagle Pointe, Brunson Estates, and SH-146 corridor HOA proposal so the homeowner has the paperwork ready for the review board. We have cleared review at major Baytown-area HOAs multiple times and are familiar with the specific grass-variety standards each enforces.'),
				array('question'=>'What does a typical Baytown sod install cost?','answer'=>'Baytown sod pricing is driven primarily by aggressive decompaction and coastal-clay amendment scope, drainage corrections, and HOA documentation requirements. As a reference: typical Baytown residential front-yard re-sods with full decompaction and amendment range from $3,000 to $6,500. Full-yard quarter-acre installs typically run $5,500 to $13,000. Pool-and-patio installs typically run $8,000 to $18,000. Storm-and-hurricane recovery installs with drainage redesign typically run $10,000 to $25,000+. Refinery-adjacent commercial and industrial projects along SH-146 and I-10 East are quoted as custom commercial bids with full SWPPP and refinery-adjacent insurance documentation. Every Baytown proposal includes line-item pricing.'),
