			'show' => array('st_augustine'=>'1','bermuda'=>'0','zoysia'=>'1'),
			'faqs' => array(
				array('question'=>'Why is The Woodlands install standard so much higher than other Houston markets?','answer'=>'Because The Woodlands is the premium-standard residential market in north Houston, and the architectural review committee enforces that standard rigorously. The Woodlands Development Standards Committee reviews every visible turf replacement, enforces specific grass-variety standards, and requires modification request paperwork for any install. Beyond the paperwork, the actual install standard is higher: canopy limb-up, surface-root pruning, pH-corrected organic amendment, and Woodlands-approved variety selection are all non-negotiable on every Woodlands install we deliver.'),
				array('question'=>'What grass works best in The Woodlands?','answer'=>'On almost every established Woodlands lot under mature pine and oak canopy (Grogan\'s Mill, Cochran\'s Crossing, Indian Springs, older Carlton Woods and Sterling Ridge filings), Palmetto St. Augustine — it handles the 3–5 hours of filtered light most established Woodlands lots get, tolerates the acidic pH after years of pine-needle drop, and clears WDSC architectural review without friction. On premium pool-adjacent and signature visible zones in Carlton Woods and Sterling Ridge, Zeon Zoysia is the premium upgrade. Bermuda fails almost universally under Woodlands canopy; we steer homeowners away from it on the front-edge estimate.'),
				array('question'=>'Why does my Woodlands lawn keep thinning out?','answer'=>'Three reasons working together that are more pronounced in The Woodlands than anywhere else in Houston: insufficient filtered light (your canopy has matured over decades and your lawn has been losing light gradually), surface tree feeder roots in the upper 4–6 inches of soil competing aggressively with grass roots, and 30+ years of accumulated pine-needle and leaf litter driving soil pH dramatically downward. The Woodlands-standard fix is the whole package: canopy limb-up, surface-root pruning, pH-corrected organic amendment, and the right shade-tolerant variety. Without all of those steps, even Palmetto St. Augustine fails within two seasons.'),
				array('question'=>'Will The Woodlands approve my re-sod?','answer'=>'Yes — provided the grass variety, install method, and timing match the WDSC architectural standards. We ship a pre-filled Woodlands modification request package with every proposal so the homeowner has the paperwork ready for the review board. We have cleared WDSC review at Carlton Woods, Sterling Ridge, Grogan\'s Mill, Cochran\'s Crossing, Indian Springs, Creekside Park, and Alden Bridge — multiple times across each village.'),
				array('question'=>'Do I really need surface-root pruning on a Woodlands install?','answer'=>'On established Woodlands lots with mature pine and oak canopy, yes — and it is the step contractors most commonly skip. Mature tree feeder roots radiate within 4–6 inches of the soil surface for 40+ feet of horizontal range from the tree trunk. New sod laid over those feeder roots is in immediate competition for water and nutrients on day one, and the sod loses unless we sever the feeder roots in the lawn zone at install. Without that step, even Palmetto St. Augustine struggles within two seasons. We include surface-root pruning in standard scope on every established Woodlands lot.'),
				array('question'=>'Why does pH amendment matter in The Woodlands?','answer'=>'Because 30+ years of accumulated pine-needle and leaf litter under most Woodlands lots has driven soil pH meaningfully downward — we routinely measure pH below 5.5 on established Woodlands installs, where the optimal range for St. Augustine is 6.0–7.0. Acidic pH locks up nutrients and prevents healthy root development even when amendment and irrigation are otherwise correct. We soil-test every Woodlands install and add lime to correct pH where the test indicates it. This step is non-negotiable on most established Woodlands lots.'),
				array('question'=>'Do you handle Woodlands Waterway and commercial work?','answer'=>'Yes — Woodlands commercial work along the Waterway, Market Street, and Hughes Landing corridors is a meaningful part of our commercial book. We deliver premium sod across pedestrian-visible frontage and engineered hydroseed across back zones, all built to Woodlands premium-standard specifications with WDSC approval. We are bonded and insured to Woodlands commercial standards, and we ship full bid-document submittal packages with every commercial Woodlands proposal.'),
				array('question'=>'What does a typical Woodlands sod install cost?','answer'=>'Woodlands sod pricing reflects the Woodlands-standard install scope — canopy work, surface-root pruning, pH-corrected amendment, Woodlands-approved variety, and WDSC paperwork are all built into standard pricing. As a reference: typical Woodlands front-yard re-sods range from $4,500 to $9,000 depending on lot size and canopy density. Full-yard quarter-acre to half-acre installs typically run $9,000 to $20,000. Premium Carlton Woods, Sterling Ridge, and Waterway-adjacent estate installs with Zeon Zoysia typically run $18,000 to $40,000+. Every Woodlands proposal includes line-item pricing.'),
			),
