				array('question'=>'Will Telfair, Riverstone, Sweetwater, Greatwood, Avalon, or First Colony approve my re-sod?','answer'=>'Yes — provided the grass variety, install method, and timing match each community\'s architectural standards. We ship a pre-filled HOA modification request package with every Sugar Land proposal. We have cleared review at every major Sugar Land master-planned community multiple times and know exactly which varieties each accepts.'),
				array('question'=>'Why does every Sugar Land lawn need a soil amendment?','answer'=>'Because Sugar Land sits on expansive clay similar to neighboring Pearland and Missouri City. The clay is dense, slow-draining, and aggressive in its swell-and-shrink cycle. Sod laid directly on raw Sugar Land clay cannot push roots into the surface and fails within 18–24 months. Every successful Sugar Land install includes a 2–3 inch sandy loam topsoil amendment before sod goes down.'),
				array('question'=>'What grass works best in Sugar Land?','answer'=>'On 80%+ of Sugar Land lots, Palmetto St. Augustine — it handles the partial canopy most established lots have, tolerates Sugar Land expansive clay over a properly amended surface, and clears HOA architectural review at every Sugar Land master-planned community. On full-sun newer-construction front yards in Telfair and Riverstone we install Tifway 419 Bermuda. Premium Zeon Zoysia on pool-and-patio installs.'),
				array('question'=>'Why does new builder sod keep dying in Telfair and Riverstone?','answer'=>'Because the production builder lays Raleigh St. Augustine directly over raw expansive Sugar Land clay with no amendment layer and no drainage correction. It looks great on closing day and fails within 18–24 months. We correct this exact pattern with the standard Sugar Land scope — strip, decompact, amend with 2–3 inches of sandy loam, regrade, and reinstall with Palmetto St. Augustine.'),
